finite verb)의 판별","명사절을 이끄는 접속사 that과 완전한 문장의 결합","수식어구가 길게 이어진 구조에서 복수 주어와 동사의 수일치"],"tags":["어법","명사절 접속사","정동사와 준동사","수일치","도치구문"],"difficulty":"BASIC","markedExpressions":[{"label":"(A)","expression":"as","isError":false,"errorExpression":"as","surroundingText":"of glass is, frankly, a bit of a mess. Clear and perfect as it might appear to our eyes, down at the","pointCode":"l"},{"label":"(B)","expression":"see","isError":false,"errorExpression":"see","surroundingText":"misshapen sheets of glass you sometimes see in old stained-glass windows, thicker at the bottom","pointCode":"a"},{"label":"(C)","expression":"are","isError":false,"errorExpression":"are","surroundingText":"thicker at the bottom than the top, are generally not that way because the glass is slowly","pointCode":"d"},{"label":"(D)","expression":"has endured","isError":false,"errorExpression":"has endured","surroundingText":"sinking over time. Unless the church has endured temperatures of more than 400˚C, the chances","pointCode":"d"},{"label":"(E)","expression":"struggle","isError":true,"correction":"struggle","errorExpression":"struggling","surroundingText":"oldest human-made substances, scientists still struggle to comprehend why glass behaves the way it does.","pointCode":"a"}],"options":[{"label":"(A)","text":"as"},{"label":"(B)","text":"see"},{"label":"(C)","text":"are"},{"label":"(D)","text":"has endured"},{"label":"(E)","text":"struggling"}],"wrongOptionExplanations":{"(A)":"'Clear and perfect as' 구문에서 'as'는 양보의 의미('~이지만')를 나타내는 접속사로, 보어인 형용사들이 앞으로 도치된 양보 양식의 절을 올바르게 이끌고 있습니다.","(B)":"주어 'those misshapen sheets of glass'를 수식하는 관계사절 내에서 주어 'you' 뒤에 쓰인 일반동사의 현재형 'see'는 구조상 적절합니다.","(D)":"조건의 부사절을 이끄는 'Unless' 내에 단수 명사 'the church'를 주어로 삼아 현재완료 능동태 동사인 'has endured'로 쓰인 것은 문맥과 수일치에 어긋나지 않습니다.","(C)":"문장의 주어 'Those misshapen sheets of glass'에 연결되는 정동사 자리로, 관계사절과 삽입형 수식어구가 길게 연결되어 있지만 주어가 복수형이므로 복수 동사 'are'는 수일치에 잘 부합합니다."},"correctAnswers":["(E)"],"passageWithMarkers":"The structure of glass is, frankly, a bit of a mess. Clear and perfect __(A) as__ it might appear to our eyes, down at the molecular level, glass looks more like a random ball-pit of atoms. The technical term for this mess depends on who you're asking: for some scientists it is an \"amorphous solid,\" for others a \"supercooled liquid.\" In theory it's both liquid and solid, though, given the way it behaves, in practice it's really the latter. In fact, at room temperature glass never behaves as a liquid, even an imperceptibly viscous one, even over long periods of time (though it can \"sweat\" if you don't add enough lime to the mix). Those misshapen sheets of glass you sometimes __(B) see__ in old stained-glass windows, thicker at the bottom than the top, __(C) are__ generally not that way because the glass is slowly sinking over time. Unless the church __(D) has endured__ temperatures of more than 400˚C, the chances are they are uneven because that's the way they were blown and solidified in the first place. Flat glass was only developed in the nineteenth century and we had to wait until the middle of the twentieth century for truly flat, thin sheets of glass. The paradox is that, despite it being one of the very oldest human-made substances, scientists still __(E) struggling__ to comprehend why glass behaves the way it does. Glass seems to obey most molecular laws. As one glassmaker put it, glass is not a material; it's a state.","_typeId":"GRAMMAR_ERROR","_typeLabel":"어법 판단","_generationPlan":"STANDARD"}}
